# Performans bütçesi — kazanılanı ayda bir değil, her değişiklikte koru

Boss rule (Kaan, 2026-10-17, after PERF-SAGLAMLIK-2026-10): these numbers are not a
one-time trophy. The job is to never need a full re-audit to notice they slipped.
**Read and follow** `.cursor/skills/performans-butcesi-koruma/SKILL.md` before closing
any change in scope below.

## Trigger

Any change touching: `app/dashboard/doktor/hastalar/[id]/**` (patient file), any
`app/api/**` route on a path a doctor or patient waits on synchronously (portal,
session, note, task sync), `gorevEkle`/`gorevSenkron` or any similar per-item sync
loop, a query against `hasta_lab_sonuclari` / `hasta_goruntulemeler` / `hasta_belgeler`
/ `kontrasepsiyon` or any other hot patient-file table, or any new call to an external
service (email, WhatsApp, calendar, ElevenLabs, a new provider).

## Hard ceilings (measured baseline → budget, not the goal to re-hit)

| Metric | 2026-10-17 measured | Warn above | Fail above |
|---|---|---|---|
| `hastalar/[id]` page JS (`next build`) | 18.6 kB | 30 kB | 60 kB |
| `hastalar/[id]` First Load JS | 214 kB | 260 kB | 350 kB |
| Shared JS bundle (all routes) | 88.9 kB | — | any route's own First Load JS moves >10 kB vs. its last recorded number |
| Portal/API GET hot-path latency, `SahteVeritabani` harness median | 62.3 ms | 100 ms | 200 ms |
| Per-item sync round trips (`gorevEkle`/`gorevSenkron` pattern) | 2 (batched, independent of N) | — | **fail**: any code path doing one round trip per item again (O(N) instead of O(1)/batched) |
| Hot-path query plan, `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, COSTS)` on the four tables above | Index Scan, cost 2.37 | — | **fail**: plan reverts to Seq Scan (cost ≈17+) — means the covering index was dropped, renamed, or the query shape no longer matches it |
| External call timeout coverage | 100% (every external fetch has one) | — | **fail**: any new or touched external call ships with no explicit timeout |

These are ceilings, not targets — getting faster than the measured baseline is fine and
is not a regression to explain.

## What closing a change in scope requires

1. Bundle: `next build` output for the touched route(s), compared to the ceiling above.
2. Latency: run the existing `SahteVeritabani` harness on the touched route if it's a
   hot API path; compare to the ceiling.
3. Round trips: if the change touches a per-item sync/loop, count round trips for
   N=1 and N=5; it must stay flat (batched), not scale with N.
4. Query plan: if the change touches a query against one of the four hot tables (or
   adds a new hot-read path), run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, COSTS)` and confirm an Index Scan.
5. Timeouts: any new external call gets an explicit timeout and a fallback path before
   merge — no exceptions, no "add it later" entry.
6. Country parity: per `ulke-paritesi-duzeltme`, the same check runs wherever the
   touched code is shared with the six country branches, not just on `main`.
7. Append one line to `docs/audits/PERF-BUTCE-LOG.md` — date, change, metric(s)
   touched, measured number, pass/warn/fail. A warn can close with a one-line reason;
   a fail cannot close without either a fix or Kaan's explicit written acceptance of
   the regression (with his reason) in the same log line.

## Why a log, not a monthly audit

A full audit (bundle analyzer across all routes, harness across all hot paths, EXPLAIN
on every table, grep for every external call) is the right way to *establish* a
baseline, not to *maintain* one. Re-running it monthly means regressions sit live for
weeks before anyone notices. This rule moves the check to the one moment it's cheap:
the change that could have caused it, while the diff is still in hand.

